  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nA morning cold front passage has brought slightly cooler \ntemperatures to the area today, as compared to yesterday. With \nthat said, high temperatures are still forecast in the upper 80s \nto 90s today. Along with the hot temperatures, breezy conditions \nremain over eastern Montana behind the morning cold front passage.\nLook for these winds to temporarily decrease this afternoon and \nevening before another, stronger, cold front moves through the \narea tonight into Monday. This front will bring additional windy \nconditions to much of the area late this evening through Monday. \nWhile northwest to northeasterly peak wind gusts in the 20s and \n30s mph will be the most common across the area, a few locally \nstronger gusts are possible over far eastern Montana into western \nNorth Dakota and South Dakota and over far southern Montana into \nnorthern Wyoming in the Bighorn Basin and Bighorn Canyon. These \nwinds will decrease Monday evening and night across the area. \n\nCritical fire weather conditions continue today with the hot, \ndry, and breezy conditions. Much cooler temperatures and higher \nrelative humidity values will limit the fire weather risk Monday, \nbut the wind will still lead to elevated fire weather conditions \nacross much of the area. The one area where critical fire weather \nconditions are expected to persist through Monday is over portions\nof far eastern Montana into western North Dakota and South \nDakota. Please avoid causing a spark during this time, and if you \nsee a wildfire, report it.\n\nTemperatures will be much cooler Monday and Tuesday with high \ntemperatures in the upper 60s to lower 80s and low temperatures in\nthe 40s and 50s. For those camping in the mountains, low \ntemperatures are forecast to fall into the 30s Monday night and \nTuesday night as low as 7000 feet. Temperatures will then trend \nwarmer Wednesday into the weekend as low amplitude ridging builds \nover the region. As of now, Friday looks to be the warmest day \nwith high temperatures back in the 90s to near 100 degrees. There \nis even a medium chance of exceeding 100 degrees again over the \nplains of eastern Montana Friday afternoon. While uncertain still,\na cold front may break down the ridge this weekend, bringing \nslightly cooler temperatures back to the region. Will have to see \nhow the forecast evolves through the week. \n\nThere is a slight chance of showers over central Montana this \nevening into tonight. With this, a weak thunderstorm cannot be \nrule out during the evening, but the chance is low (15 percent). \nOtherwise, mostly dry conditions will exist this week. By late \nweekend into the following week, there is a signal for the return \nof monsoonal moisture and energy over the region. This could, if \nit occurs, re- introduce a daily chance of showers and \nthunderstorms to the forecast. Something to keep an eye on. LA",
